  src/main/java/io/github/hecbatista/metalshaders/render/EntityTakeover.java (prewarm) compiles
  pipelines while the world loads, following Vitrail's docs/internals/game-graphics-api.md
  ("precompile every pipeline with its real source before the first pass"); which pipelines
  is our own measured choice.

  src/main/java/io/github/hecbatista/metalshaders/mixin/sodium/RenderRegionMixin.java keeps a
  batch map for the light because of Vitrail's finding (docs/sky-and-shadows.md, "Culling for
  the light"): a batch is kept until its region's list changes, so a shadow draw sharing the
  camera's batches draws the camera's holes.

  src/main/java/io/github/hecbatista/metalshaders/glsl/ProgramTranslator.java translates a
  vertex and fragment stage together, as one program: one uniform block holding the union of
  both stages' uniforms in a fixed order, and one shared table of in/out locations by name,
  with a matrix taking one location per column. This design is described in Vitrail's
  docs/translation.md ("The unit of translation is the program, not the file"). No Vitrail
  code was read or copied for it.

  src/main/java/io/github/hecbatista/metalshaders/pack/RenderTargetPlan.java and
  PackDirectives.java follow rules described in Vitrail's docs/internals/render-targets.md:
  format directives are found inside comments but only on live lines, they're folded in the
  reference's program order with final among the gbuffer programs, three-channel formats are
  promoted to four, old fixed-point formats map to 8-bit RGBA, and RGB9_E5 maps to a float
  format. The mapping table itself is ours.

  The terrain takeover (render/TerrainTakeover.java, mixin/sodium/*, mixin/
  LevelRendererTerrainMixin.java, sodium/LuxiumChunkVertex.java) follows designs described in
  Vitrail's docs/internals/terrain.md: hooking Sodium at a few points instead of rewriting its
  meshing, widening the chunk vertex with extra elements placed after Sodium's own, one pack
  program per terrain pass, the face normal by Newell's method, the tangent squared up against
  the normal with a handedness sign, and reading the block atlas NEAREST as under Iris. The
  "arithmetic road" for sampler2DShadow in glsl/ProgramTranslator.java (SHADOW_COMPARE: a plain
  sampler2D, four gathered texels compared LEQUAL and blended with the bilinear weights) follows
  Vitrail's glsl/GlslTranslator (ofShadowCompare) as described in its comments. No Vitrail code
  was copied; the GLSL and Java are ours.

  render/PackChain.java cuts the chain as Vitrail's docs/frame.md describes ("The chain is cut in
  three"): the seed and deferred passes run after the opaque world, before translucents, and the
  composite and final passes after the level; depthtex1/2 are the depth before translucents. The
  game's own translucent phase is drawn onto a separate image and composed onto colortex0
  afterwards (frame.md, "Where the seed does not reach"). mixin/sodium/ChunkVertexMixin.java
  carries the block id on Sodium's vertex object through copyVertexTo, as Vitrail's
  ChunkVertexMixin does. No Vitrail code was copied.

  render/LevelPass.java (the game's "Main" pass made one that can step aside and reopen) and
  render/EntityTakeover.java (opening the pack's pass over a run of served draws inside
  RenderTypeFeatureRenderer.executeGroup, the level's solid-feature window) follow designs
  described in Vitrail's render/LevelPass and docs/internals/entities.md ("The door is a group of
  draws"). No Vitrail code was copied.

  The shadow pass (render/TerrainTakeover.drawShadow, render/ShadowTargets.java) follows Vitrail's
  docs/sky-and-shadows.md: forward depth for the shadow map (cleared to 1, less-or-equal), the
  shadow pair as the pass's fixed-function matrices, both faces drawn. Replaying the level's entities into
  the map and dropping what the shadow program doesn't serve follows the same document ("What
  moves is submitted a second time, for the light"; "inside the light's walk a refusal is a drop").

